COMPARE='''28,44~Which comparison distinguishes ventilation from diffusion?~Ventilation moves air in bulk; diffusion moves gases down their own gradients~Both mean only oxygen binding to heme|Diffusion is always the same as chest expansion~Airflow and molecular gas exchange are related but distinct processes.
28,44~Which pairing correctly distinguishes external and internal respiration?~External: alveoli and blood; internal: blood and tissues~External: bone and cartilage; internal: nose and pharynx|Both occur only inside the trachea~External respiration is pulmonary gas exchange, while internal respiration is systemic tissue exchange.
6,16~Which structure belongs to the respiratory zone rather than the conducting zone?~Respiratory bronchiole~Terminal bronchiole|Trachea~Respiratory bronchioles have alveoli and participate in exchange; terminal bronchioles are conducting structures.
8~Compared with mouth breathing, what usual nasal function conditions air more effectively?~Greater warming, humidification, and particle filtering~Direct oxygen loading of every red cell|Elimination of the need for alveoli~The nasal cavity provides extensive conditioned contact with inhaled air.
9~Which pharyngeal region normally serves mainly as an air passage?~Nasopharynx~Oropharynx|Laryngopharynx~The nasopharynx is an airway, whereas the lower pharyngeal regions are shared with swallowing pathways.
9,10~Which comparison separates pharynx and larynx?~Pharynx is the throat passage; larynx is the cartilaginous voice box~Both are alveolar sacs|The larynx is the same structure as the esophagus~The larynx connects the pharyngeal region to the trachea and participates in voice and airway protection.
11~Does thyroid cartilage mean the same thing as the thyroid gland?~No, one supports the larynx and the other is an endocrine gland~Yes, both are the same tissue|Yes, both produce all surfactant~Similar names do not make the laryngeal cartilage an endocrine organ.
11,13~Which cartilage comparison is accurate?~Cricoid forms a complete ring; tracheal rings are C-shaped~Both are normally flat alveolar walls|Tracheal rings are always complete circles~The cricoid encircles the airway, while the open posterior part of tracheal rings allows flexibility near the esophagus.
12~Which fold is the main vibrating structure for ordinary voice production?~True vocal fold~Vestibular fold|Pleural fold~True vocal folds produce ordinary voiced sound; false or vestibular folds have a mainly protective role.
11,20~What separates epiglottic protection from surfactant action?~The epiglottis helps protect swallowing; surfactant reduces alveolar surface tension~Both pump blood into the lungs|Both are parts of the pleural cavity~An upper-airway flap and alveolar surface material serve different functions.
13,14~What distinguishes the trachea from the main bronchi?~The trachea is the shared windpipe before it divides into main bronchi~The trachea enters every lung lobe separately|Main bronchi are blood vessels~The trachea branches into right and left primary bronchi.
14~Which airway pairing is correct?~Primary bronchus: lung; secondary bronchus: lobe~Primary: individual alveolus; secondary: nose|Both are names for pulmonary veins~Main bronchi supply lungs; lobar bronchi supply lobes.
14~Which bronchial level supplies a bronchopulmonary segment?~Tertiary bronchus~Primary bronchus as a segment-specific name|Trachea~Tertiary bronchi are also called segmental bronchi.
15~What distinguishes bronchioles from larger bronchi?~Bronchioles lack cartilage in their walls~Bronchioles all have thicker cartilage than the trachea|Bronchioles are veins~Bronchiolar walls contain smooth muscle without the cartilage support characteristic of larger airways.
15,16~Which comes first in a selected distal airway route: terminal or respiratory bronchiole?~Terminal bronchiole~Respiratory bronchiole|They are unrelated organs~Terminal bronchioles end the conducting zone and lead to respiratory bronchioles.
17~How does an alveolar duct differ from an alveolar sac?~The duct is a passage leading among alveoli; the sac is a clustered opening region~The duct is an artery; the sac is a vein|Both are laryngeal cartilages~These terms describe successive distal airway arrangements, not pulmonary blood vessels.
17~How does an alveolus differ from an alveolar sac?~An alveolus is one air sac; an alveolar sac is a cluster~An alveolus is the whole lung|An alveolar sac is a rib muscle~Individual alveoli group around alveolar sac regions.
20~Which pairing correctly distinguishes type I and type II alveolar cells?~Type I: thin exchange surface; type II: surfactant secretion~Type I: cartilage; type II: red-cell production|Both are nasal cilia~Type I cells are thin squamous exchange cells; type II cells produce surfactant.
20~Which alveolar cell removes microbes rather than secreting most surfactant?~Alveolar macrophage~Type II alveolar cell|Type I cell as the principal phagocyte~Alveolar macrophages provide phagocytic defense; type II cells are the main surfactant source.
8,20~How do airway mucus and alveolar surfactant differ?~Mucus traps particles; surfactant reduces surface tension~Both are blood oxygen carriers|Surfactant is tracheal cartilage~The two secretions support different respiratory protective and mechanical functions.
13,18~Which comparison separates cartilage support from alveolar elasticity?~Cartilage helps support large airways; elastic fibers help lung stretch and recoil~Both perform exactly the same role in every alveolus|Alveoli normally contain C-shaped cartilage rings~Large-airway cartilage and lung elastic tissue contribute to different mechanical properties.
26~Which lobe count is usual in adult lungs?~Right: three; left: two~Right: two; left: three|Both: one~The right lung has superior, middle, and inferior lobes; the left has superior and inferior lobes.
26~Which fissure normally occurs only in the right lung?~Horizontal fissure~Oblique fissure|Both fissures are normally left-only~Both lungs have an oblique fissure, while the usual horizontal fissure is right-sided.
25~Which positional pairing is correct?~Apex: superior; base: inferior near diaphragm~Apex: inferior; base: superior|Both are inside the trachea~A lung's apex is the top region and the base rests on the diaphragm.
14,25~Which feature is a gateway for bronchi and vessels rather than space for the heart?~Pulmonary hilum~Cardiac notch|Alveolar pore~The hilum admits major structures; the cardiac notch accommodates the heart on the left.
27~Which pleural-layer pairing is accurate?~Visceral: lung surface; parietal: thoracic wall~Visceral: chest wall only; parietal: alveolar lumen|Both are tracheal cartilages~Visceral and parietal pleura line opposing sides of the pleural space.
17,27~Which air-space comparison is correct?~Alveoli normally contain air; the pleural space normally has a fluid film~Both are normally large air-filled spaces open to the atmosphere|The pleural cavity is a branch of an alveolar duct~The normal pleural cavity is a fluid-containing potential space, distinct from alveolar air spaces.
20,27~What distinguishes pleural fluid from alveolar surfactant?~Pleural fluid lubricates sliding surfaces; surfactant lowers air-liquid surface tension~Both are normally inside red cells|Pleural fluid is the main source of hemoglobin~These fluids act at different locations and have different mechanical roles.
33~Which inspiratory muscle movement pairing is accurate?~Diaphragm descends; ribs elevate with external intercostal activity~Diaphragm rises during its inspiratory contraction|Both movements decrease thoracic volume~Quiet inspiration expands the thorax through diaphragmatic and rib-cage movement.
33,35~How do quiet inspiration and quiet expiration usually differ in muscular effort?~Inspiration is active; expiration is mainly passive~Both require maximal abdominal effort|Expiration always requires a more forceful diaphragmatic contraction~Quiet exhalation mainly follows relaxation and elastic recoil.
35~Which statement distinguishes forced from quiet expiration?~Forced expiration recruits additional muscle activity~Forced expiration means no air leaves|Quiet expiration always requires maximal abdominal contraction~Abdominal and internal intercostal activity can assist forced exhalation.
34,35~Which pressure pairing correctly predicts airflow?~Alveolar below atmosphere: inward; alveolar above atmosphere: outward~Both patterns cause only inward flow|Both patterns mean no pressure gradient~Air moves from higher toward lower pressure through open airways.
29,31~What separates alveolar from intrapleural pressure?~Alveolar pressure is inside air sacs; intrapleural pressure is in the pleural space~They are always identical at every instant|Both are pressures inside systemic arteries~These pressures describe distinct compartments involved in ventilation.
30~At a normal no-flow endpoint with open airways, how does alveolar pressure compare with atmospheric pressure?~They are equal~Alveolar pressure is necessarily much lower|Alveolar pressure is necessarily much higher~Pressure equality explains the absence of net airflow at that endpoint.
32~At constant temperature for a fixed gas amount, which volume-pressure change follows Boyle's law?~Increased volume and decreased pressure~Increased volume and increased pressure|No possible relationship between volume and pressure~Boyle's law states an inverse pressure-volume relationship under the given conditions.
34,35~How does compliance differ from elastic recoil?~Compliance concerns ease of expansion; recoil concerns return after stretch~Both mean the amount of mucus coughed up|Compliance is a breathing frequency~A lung may expand readily yet have reduced recoil, as can occur in emphysema.
15,21~What distinguishes airway resistance from diffusion-barrier thickness?~Airway resistance affects bulk airflow; barrier thickness affects gas transfer~Both describe the number of lung lobes|A thicker membrane always widens bronchioles~Ventilation and diffusion can be limited at different parts of the respiratory process.
37,39~Which volume moves with a quiet breath rather than remaining after maximal exhalation?~Tidal volume~Residual volume|Neither involves air~Tidal volume is moved during quiet breathing; residual volume cannot be expelled even maximally.
38~Which reserve-volume pairing is accurate?~IRV: extra inhalation; ERV: extra exhalation~IRV: air remaining after maximal exhalation; ERV: all air in the lungs|Both are the same as respiratory rate~Reserve volumes extend beyond ordinary tidal inspiration or expiration.
40~What makes total lung capacity greater than vital capacity?~It also includes residual volume~It excludes all tidal air|It contains only dead space~TLC includes VC plus RV.
41~Which capacity remains after quiet expiration rather than being inhaled from that point?~Functional residual capacity~Inspiratory capacity|Vital capacity alone~FRC remains in the lungs; IC is the air that can be inhaled from the same resting endpoint.
6,42~Which region is anatomical dead space rather than a major gas-exchange surface?~Conducting airways~Alveolar-capillary interface|Respiratory membrane~Anatomical dead space consists of conducting passages that do not directly exchange gas.
42~Why can minute ventilation exceed alveolar ventilation?~Some moved air ventilates conducting dead space~All moved air enters blood directly|Only alveolar ventilation includes breathing frequency~Alveolar ventilation excludes the dead-space portion of each breath.
42~How does breathing depth differ from respiratory rate?~Depth concerns air per breath; rate concerns breaths per minute~Depth counts only heartbeats|Rate is a lung capacity measured after maximal inspiration~Both depth and frequency influence ventilation.
43~How does partial pressure differ from total pressure in a gas mixture?~Partial pressure belongs to one gas; total pressure sums the gas contributions~Both always mean oxygen pressure only|Total pressure counts breaths~Each constituent gas has its own partial pressure, and those contributions add to total pressure.
45~Which gas-direction pairing is normal at the alveolar interface?~Oxygen: alveoli to blood; carbon dioxide: blood to alveoli~Both normally move only from blood to alveoli|Both normally move only from alveoli to blood~Each gas diffuses down its own partial-pressure gradient at the pulmonary interface.
44~Which gas-direction pairing is normal at most systemic tissue interfaces?~Oxygen: blood to tissues; carbon dioxide: tissues to blood~Oxygen: tissues to blood; carbon dioxide: blood to tissues|Both gases enter only the airway cartilage~Systemic tissue metabolism uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide.
45~Where is most blood oxygen found compared with the smaller dissolved fraction?~Bound to hemoglobin~As free bubbles in arteries|Bound to bicarbonate~Most oxygen is carried on hemoglobin; a small amount is dissolved in plasma.
45,48~Which pairing separates oxyhemoglobin from carbaminohemoglobin?~Oxyhemoglobin carries oxygen; carbaminohemoglobin carries bound carbon dioxide~Both specifically mean carbon monoxide poisoning|Both are types of airway mucus~Oxygen binds heme while carbon dioxide binds globin groups.
46,48~Which form carries the largest share of carbon dioxide compared with direct binding to hemoglobin?~Bicarbonate~Carbaminohemoglobin|Dissolved gas only~Most carbon dioxide is carried as bicarbonate, with smaller shares bound to proteins or dissolved.
49~Which primary change distinguishes respiratory acidosis from respiratory alkalosis?~Acidosis: carbon dioxide rise; alkalosis: carbon dioxide fall~Both are defined only by a change in nasal airflow|Both always begin with low carbon dioxide~Carbon dioxide retention tends to lower pH, and excessive removal tends to raise it.
49~Why is rapid breathing not automatically hyperventilation?~Depth and metabolic carbon dioxide production also matter~Breathing rate cannot ever change|Hyperventilation only means loud breathing~Hyperventilation is excessive effective alveolar ventilation relative to carbon dioxide production.
49~Why is a slow respiratory rate not by itself proof of hypoventilation?~Breath depth and metabolic demand also affect carbon dioxide clearance~All slow breathing removes zero carbon dioxide|Rate is the only determinant of ventilation~Adequacy of ventilation depends on effective ventilation relative to metabolic production.
50,53~Which comparison distinguishes asthma from emphysema at an introductory level?~Asthma features variable airway narrowing; emphysema features alveolar destruction~Both are defined by a new lung lobe|Asthma requires every alveolar wall to be destroyed~Asthma centers on variable inflamed/narrowed airways, while emphysema involves distal structural damage.
53,54~Which comparison separates chronic bronchitis from emphysema?~Bronchitis emphasizes mucus-producing airway inflammation; emphysema emphasizes alveolar destruction~Both mean only an inflamed nasal septum|Only emphysema can contain air~The conditions can coexist in COPD but have different defining features.
34,52~Which broad distinction separates obstructive from restrictive patterns?~Obstructive limits airflow; restrictive limits expansion or lung volume~Obstructive means no alveoli exist|Restrictive always means asthma~These are physiological patterns requiring appropriate assessment, not diagnoses made from one symptom.
45~How does hypoxemia differ from tissue hypoxia?~Hypoxemia is low arterial oxygen; hypoxia is inadequate tissue oxygen availability~Both specifically mean high carbon dioxide|Hypoxia is the name of a pleural layer~Low arterial oxygen can cause tissue hypoxia, but inadequate tissue oxygenation has other causes too.
27,31~Which pleural comparison is correct?~Pneumothorax: air; pleural effusion: excess fluid~Pneumothorax: mucus; effusion: cartilage|Both are normal alveolar contents~Air and excess liquid in the pleural space are distinct problems.
45~Why can anemia reduce oxygen delivery even when dissolved oxygen pressure is normal?~There is less hemoglobin available to carry oxygen~The lungs must have no alveoli|Anemia means all carbon dioxide transport stops~Oxygen content depends strongly on hemoglobin amount, not only dissolved oxygen pressure.
55~Does finding a lung nodule automatically establish lung cancer?~No, nodules may have benign or malignant causes~Yes, every nodule is malignant|Yes, nodules always prove smoking~A nodule is an imaging finding requiring appropriate assessment rather than a diagnosis by itself.
'''
comparisons=[line.split('~') for line in COMPARE.splitlines() if line]
assert len(comparisons)==60,len(comparisons)
for s,p,a,w,e in comparisons:c([int(n) for n in s.split(',')],p,a,w,e,True)
# Short windows of selected anatomical or causal models. They do not assert
# that every omitted intermediate microscopic step is absent.
PATHS=[
([8,9],'nasal airflow through the selected upper passages',['External nares','Nasal cavity','Nasopharynx','Oropharynx','Laryngopharynx']),
([9,10,13,14],'inhaled airflow toward a lung lobe',['Laryngopharynx','Larynx','Trachea','Primary bronchus','Secondary bronchus']),
([14,15],'branching of the conducting bronchial tree',['Primary bronchus','Secondary bronchus','Tertiary bronchus','Smaller bronchioles','Terminal bronchiole']),
([15,16,17],'airflow through the selected distal airway landmarks',['Terminal bronchiole','Respiratory bronchiole','Alveolar duct','Alveolar sac','Individual alveolus']),
([20,21],'an oxygen molecule crossing the alveolar barrier toward blood',['Alveolar air','Alveolar liquid film','Type I cell layer','Thin basement-membrane region','Capillary endothelial layer']),
([28,45],'oxygenated blood leaving the pulmonary exchange region',['Pulmonary capillary blood','Pulmonary veins','Left atrium','Left ventricle','Systemic arteries']),
([28,44,45],'oxygen delivery from hemoglobin to a tissue cell',['Oxygen released from hemoglobin','Oxygen in plasma','Crossing the systemic capillary wall','Oxygen in interstitial fluid','Oxygen entering the tissue cell']),
([44,46,47],'tissue carbon dioxide reaching the bicarbonate pathway',['Carbon dioxide in a tissue cell','Carbon dioxide in interstitial fluid','Carbon dioxide in plasma','Carbon dioxide entering a red cell','Bicarbonate formation in the red cell']),
([44,48],'a selected carbamino transport route from tissues',['Tissue carbon dioxide release','Carbon dioxide in systemic plasma','Entry into a red cell','Attachment to globin groups','Venous transport as carbaminohemoglobin']),
([47,58],'tissue bicarbonate production and export',['Carbon dioxide enters a red cell','Carbonic-anhydrase-assisted hydration','Carbonic acid formation','Dissociation into hydrogen and bicarbonate','Bicarbonate export to plasma']),
([45,47,58],'bicarbonate-derived carbon dioxide unloading in the lungs',['Bicarbonate reenters a red cell','Carbonic acid is reformed','Carbon dioxide is regenerated','Carbon dioxide enters pulmonary plasma','Carbon dioxide diffuses into alveolar air']),
([33,34],'a simple quiet-inspiration model',['Inspiratory muscle activation','Diaphragm descent','Thoracic expansion','Alveolar pressure below atmosphere','Air entering the lungs']),
([35,36],'a simple quiet-expiration model',['Inspiratory muscle relaxation','Elastic recoil','Alveolar volume reduction','Alveolar pressure above atmosphere','Air leaving the lungs']),
([35],'a selected forced-expiration mechanism',['Abdominal muscle contraction','Abdominal pressure increase','Diaphragm pushed upward','Thoracic volume reduction','Outward airflow']),
([15],'bronchiolar narrowing with unchanged driving pressure and other factors',['Bronchiolar muscle contraction','Airway diameter reduction','Airway resistance increase','Airflow reduction','Reduced fresh-air delivery downstream']),
([20],'a simplified surfactant-loss mechanism',['Surfactant reduction','Surface tension increase','Increased alveolar collapse tendency','Less aerated exchange surface','Impaired gas exchange']),
([50,51],'one airway-edema mechanism in asthma',['Airway inflammation','Mucosal edema','Lumen narrowing','Resistance to airflow increases','Airflow becomes limited']),
([53],'one gas-exchange mechanism of emphysema with other factors unchanged',['Alveolar wall injury','Loss of alveolar partitions','Reduced gas-exchange surface area','Reduced diffusion capacity','Impaired oxygen uptake']),
([49],'a primary sustained hyperventilation sequence',['Alveolar ventilation exceeds metabolic need','Carbon dioxide removal exceeds production','Arterial carbon dioxide falls','Hydrogen-ion concentration tends to fall','Blood pH tends to rise']),
([49],'a primary sustained hypoventilation sequence',['Alveolar ventilation is insufficient','Carbon dioxide retention','Arterial carbon dioxide rises','Hydrogen-ion concentration tends to rise','Blood pH tends to fall']),
]
